#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078367 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078517 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "972bb75e-a787-58f1-83c0-1ae32aaf925d" :knowledge-point/title "Dilating Points in the Coordinate Plane"}] :question/id #uuid "c2883f5a-2126-51d9-b7bd-04bfd4418a3d" :question/math-academy-id "e-7841" :question/problem "Find the image of the point $(-5,2)$ under the action of a dilation with center $O$ and scale factor $-3?$" :question/worked-solution "A dilation with center at the origin and a scale factor of $-3$ can be represented by the function\n\n$$\n(x,y) \\mapsto (-3x,-3y).\n$$\n\nApplying this function to the point $(-5,2),$ we have\n\n$$\n(-5,2) \\mapsto \\big((-3) \\cdot (-5), \\: (-3) \\cdot 2 \\big) = (15,-6).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resulting point is $(15,-6).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078368 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078518 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "261176ed-19bf-5d71-86b7-ba2e0ba31dc1" :knowledge-point/title "Dilating Figures in the Coordinate Plane"}] :question/id #uuid "67b906da-2da2-570c-8a11-de5839c7686a" :question/math-academy-id "e-7842" :question/problem "![](images/83/83e7c4533710e42e16cbe883188f619321914815d905344547bd6c81d253a3ac.png)\n\nWhat is the image of the polygon shown above under a dilation that is centered at the origin and has a scale factor of $-2?$" :question/worked-solution "The dilation with center at the origin and a scale factor of $-2$ can be represented by the function\n\n$$\n(x,y) \\mapsto \\left(-2x,-2y\\right).\n$$\n\nApplying this function to the vertices $(2,1),$ $(3,1),$ $(3,3),$  $(1,3),$ $(1,2),$  and $(2,2),$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2,1) & ↦(-2\\cdot 2,\\,-2\\cdot 1)=(-4,-2) \\\\ (3,1) & ↦(-2\\cdot 3,\\,-2\\cdot 1)=(-6,-2) \\\\ (3,3) & ↦(-2\\cdot 3,\\,-2\\cdot 3)=(-6,-6) \\\\ (1,3) & ↦(-2\\cdot 1,\\,-2\\cdot 3)=(-2,-6) \\\\ (1,2) & ↦(-2\\cdot 1,\\,-2\\cdot 2)=(-2,-4) \\\\ (2,2) & ↦(-2\\cdot 2,\\,-2\\cdot 2)=(-4,-4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following polygon.\n\n![](images/a3/a3ce2ccd16db3e9013947b475bbca5fec1d34ff5e10414eff426e8389271c219.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078369 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078519 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f22cdd47-6fd4-5f15-b5a9-ce290e204a57" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Scale Factor of a Dilation in the Coordinate Plane"}] :question/id #uuid "7c567754-a661-5235-bde3-ae6cbff72ed5" :question/math-academy-id "e-7843" :question/problem "![](images/cd/cd07f68993f7e151e2b24fe9305b726ce40b9bc5f56399d6616e46c83f90cc87.png)\n\n \n\nThe point $P'(-2,-4)$ is the image of the point $P(1,2)$ under a dilation with the center at the origin, as shown above. What is the scale factor of the transformation?" :question/worked-solution "A dilation with center at the origin and a factor of $k$ can be represented by the function \n\n$$\n(x,y) \\mapsto (kx,ky)\\,.\n$$\n\nApplying this function to the point $P$ and equating the result to $P',$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(k\\cdot 1,k\\cdot 2)=(-2,-4)\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}k=-2 \\\\ 2k=-4.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=\\dfrac{(-4)}{2}=-2.$\n\n![](images/21/219980e6dee08094db21c153f37b107d6fd85709aa50cf8c546a5ba050aac567.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078373 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078520 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c2ce690-f8a9-5cc2-9baf-2d7163602589" :knowledge-point/title "The Image of a Line Under a Dilation"}] :question/id #uuid "b0ca5c59-6d06-5b5a-8b25-bd364fcb4bf5" :question/math-academy-id "e-8547" :question/problem "![](images/94/94ff7096b1befc0ce2538efc345ff3f090c66ee28f98bf62b6162cfed9226137.png)\n\nA dilation with center $O$ is defined by the function $(x,y)  \\mapsto \\left(\\dfrac53x, \\dfrac53y\\right).$  What is the image of the line shown above under the action of this transformation?" :question/worked-solution "A dilation maps a line $l$ not passing through the center of the dilation onto a parallel line $l'.$ \n\nTo find the image of the line, we proceed as follows: \n\n- Take a point $B \\in l.$\n\n- Find the image $B'$ of $B$ under the dilation.\n\n- Find $l'$ as the line that passes through $B'$ and is parallel to $l.$\n\nNotice that $B(-3,0)$ lies on $l,$ as shown below. The image of this point under the dilation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{5}{3}\\cdot (-3),\\frac{5}{3}\\cdot 0) & =(-5,0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following image of $l\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/7e/7eca5ac26e32d153f69f67076100c7c1ebc26efe26d5b1603ed459a9101f93db.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535953 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078517 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "972bb75e-a787-58f1-83c0-1ae32aaf925d" :knowledge-point/title "Dilating Points in the Coordinate Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3386990f-4a12-5bfb-bca6-dc705891815e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f4/f4f6a383a5aced3a181ad9b910a5e3990ee9a2febe2af6a8f27bb53a32f07c32.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535512} {:answer/id #uuid "7329dedf-72c5-5967-ba9a-7094f5e60b60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b8/b84aa56a8e238e80e673ebec28dc9de1463f32f07fe0163650905e56a9ec0765.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535614} {:answer/id #uuid "855a8c4a-da91-54ff-97cc-06728b6eaf2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/0a/0ab97722306686003c288a1dbf39038f0db6d28bedd6d92bb7626dbf18a637b2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535647} {:answer/id #uuid "ddcee905-986e-51be-9e24-d58126a44580" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/cb/cbbf6d9ef8a860214bf8ec1ae867184f96f783ea39288c68483af7984c3e0b1b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535775} {:answer/id #uuid "f9b70be3-592d-5923-9391-fcfd435fd44b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8c/8cb47f7a93ce259ff03e04ecd848dd199edf26f357c8c82da29449302728187e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535820}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ddcee905-986e-51be-9e24-d58126a44580" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/cb/cbbf6d9ef8a860214bf8ec1ae867184f96f783ea39288c68483af7984c3e0b1b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535775} :answer-field/id #uuid "00281969-c87d-5b23-a09d-d3467e250aad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535833}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5e9dde9d-e070-5830-aa05-46ebbed13863" :question/math-academy-id "q-125558" :question/problem "![](images/b8/b8730b1936d1ff9f0c8d3845a70327e351b23c7085f248bcb6e99e73f0cb8c9e.png)\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the point $(8,-4)$ under the action of a dilation with center $O$ and scale factor $\\frac{1}{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "A dilation with center at the origin and a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$ can be represented by the function  \n\n$$\n(x,y)↦(\\frac{1}{2}x,\\frac{1}{2}y).\n$$\n\nApplying this function to the point $(8,-4),$ we have\n\n$$\n(8,-4)↦(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 8,\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-4))=(4,-2).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resulting point is $(4,-2).$\n\n![](images/c2/c2c00f1380ce776da3fe4e1ab2b30c969d5d7436966d907db7a0ceb5f0a13f33.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535992 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078517 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "972bb75e-a787-58f1-83c0-1ae32aaf925d" :knowledge-point/title "Dilating Points in the Coordinate Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2612f47d-0452-52a5-b697-b1b879af646c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d2/d238f021b9afd090dbc768cae0a6516969a0b281e88175889c5c616874739f10.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535494} {:answer/id #uuid "2e1b842b-03ba-5e9b-84c1-8c4e10efb822" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d6/d6c03d6d7cf77a6cf53bef74f08b823c4c53e60fa500aabeeead4b9fe6aac6d9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535503} {:answer/id #uuid "3604c806-fdc9-5cbd-952d-ce15ba543740" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/44/44e6342a13efb1aff9a223a48c5da9ba41d04a5a2944fdf4bdb08b3ff947c47f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535517} {:answer/id #uuid "8f24f992-4e40-57bc-8429-0ac08457c506" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d5/d535d5bb169ecd50b41de6900dca3fbaa3314f1b510145b0266b680e60f22179.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535664} {:answer/id #uuid "d7270f80-e748-5db4-a204-f36a17d9bcea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1b/1b6adf3c5a87eee1dc41b4b8bfe10303f8f11d9c84e992e71d51f62cb3cdd363.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535764}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f24f992-4e40-57bc-8429-0ac08457c506" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d5/d535d5bb169ecd50b41de6900dca3fbaa3314f1b510145b0266b680e60f22179.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535664} :answer-field/id #uuid "275fc850-3e33-59ce-9392-09c44d4021e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535845}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cac69546-49cc-507e-802a-92a175e045ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-125574" :question/problem "![](images/84/841dfcfabb27fe93002cb05c8d84ad9da2e0a9f6b810476e7964651e515e757c.png)\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the point $(8,-6)$ under the action of a dilation with center $O$ and scale factor $\\frac{1}{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "A dilation with center at the origin and a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$ can be represented by the function\n\n$$\n(x,y)↦(\\frac{1}{2}x,\\frac{1}{2}y).\n$$\n\nApplying this function to the point $(8,-6),$ we have\n\n$$\n(8,-6)↦(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 8,\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-6))=(4,-3).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resulting point is $(4,-3).$\n\n![](images/53/53df2214415b81380c96af98b2d073e2a16480715386926603cd471eec66dec6.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535980 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078518 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "261176ed-19bf-5d71-86b7-ba2e0ba31dc1" :knowledge-point/title "Dilating Figures in the Coordinate Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a665b85-1226-5bdb-bb70-29b874a55538" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/70/70ab8c2fe67dbf76952995240d2bdcf102111c2ce8cfe91850886e9a439dcd45.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535475} {:answer/id #uuid "c24f84de-5c00-518c-ab34-899bc789aed5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/63/6390b39e47b76519fe13b4cb4ed8a666ec45ff81a3f26d5d737ba11e7b8a2fa1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535732} {:answer/id #uuid "cbef1f47-0b08-587d-9e20-b7fbcb525255" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/90/904c2c513ad7899b0c031b469d4f0d3505cc95d5e365c9437408e6f9828906fa.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535741} {:answer/id #uuid "f3b6343b-0aab-5a56-b101-de38eae283b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c5/c5acbc87cedde74b975d86c528d66bd779dfc72778d178fc8eacca09c7ff9ad9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535809} {:answer/id #uuid "f91e2358-9481-5e26-8eb3-f2aff1529abc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1a/1aebfca6500abd5c7298492121f0c087c2c1861ee4bd770c27867d7e37fb631a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535817}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f91e2358-9481-5e26-8eb3-f2aff1529abc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1a/1aebfca6500abd5c7298492121f0c087c2c1861ee4bd770c27867d7e37fb631a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535817} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa59387b-3aeb-5780-923e-0243d4eb6960" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535893}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a76fa1c3-fa48-5812-bf8b-fa26865dd75b" :question/math-academy-id "q-125692" :question/problem "![](images/98/98e64a245737736bfc987fe9230b1ee3fc81eef4aedfea8c2e5e9bcf949f8203.png)\n\nWhat is the image of the polygon shown above under a dilation that is centered at the origin and has a scale factor of $-\\frac{2}{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "The dilation with center at the origin and a scale factor of $-\\frac{2}{3}$ can be represented by the function\n\n$$\n(x,y)↦(-\\frac{2}{3}x,-\\frac{2}{3}y).\n$$\n\nApplying this function to the vertices $(-3,-3),$ $(0,0),$  and $(-3,3),$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-3,-3) & ↦(-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (-3),\\,-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (-3))=(2,2) \\\\ (0,0) & ↦(-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 0,\\,-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 0)=(0,0) \\\\ (-3,3) & ↦(-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (-3),\\,-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 3)=(2,-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following polygon.\n\n![](images/29/2921272ffd1c38c082bf2123086d8c7b19a910c91d132810042b86728b820f8d.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535927 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078519 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f22cdd47-6fd4-5f15-b5a9-ce290e204a57" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Scale Factor of a Dilation in the Coordinate Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65f2d77a-2b81-5d83-98b3-76968aebd21b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535595} {:answer/id #uuid "98f7f5ac-92f2-57d4-9f68-6f000ab5715b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535675} {:answer/id #uuid "ac7ae81f-0d85-559d-8d81-82c0fa4336b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535702} {:answer/id #uuid "c9fc64ad-4252-5b4b-bd30-9d46db806f36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535738} {:answer/id #uuid "e0d75dd9-891a-59ff-9bb8-4426ce367ae3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535782}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac7ae81f-0d85-559d-8d81-82c0fa4336b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535702} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8a55fbf-ed96-50fd-a31b-6db6c54e9cf1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535912}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0b671036-c3a4-5237-8125-3af3f125754a" :question/math-academy-id "q-125779" :question/problem "![](images/14/14ca2d4167ff6950d5a9d7bb41cc856ecbc211a033518b8b366ac977953700b1.png)\n\nThe point ${P}^{′}(3,1.5)$ is the image of the point $P(-4,-2)$ under a dilation with the center at the origin, as shown above. What is the scale factor of the transformation?" :question/worked-solution "A dilation with center at the origin and a factor of $k$ can be represented by the function\n\n$$\n(x,y)↦(kx,ky)\\,.\n$$\n\nApplying this function to the point $P$ and equating the result to ${P}^{′},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(k\\cdot (-4),k\\cdot (-2)=(3,1.5)\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}-4k=3 \\\\ -2k=1.5.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=\\frac{3}{-4}=\\frac{1.5}{-2}=-\\frac{3}{4}.$   \n\n![](images/d3/d3b818151bcfddd3fd4f642463ba4e06ba1844d4b772652dadde0d3049e441ee.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535948 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078519 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f22cdd47-6fd4-5f15-b5a9-ce290e204a57" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Scale Factor of a Dilation in the Coordinate Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e312727-bada-52b7-812c-8e09bc0642fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,0),\\,\\,k=-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535481} {:answer/id #uuid "4d5909d8-e636-596d-bf60-0dffd5c4f965" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,2),\\,\\,k=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535549} {:answer/id #uuid "4f9a4d27-439e-5b3c-aa98-588adc4b66e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,0),\\,\\,k=\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535554} {:answer/id #uuid "ccd6096f-fbbc-553e-a9fe-bc9586b939ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,1),\\,\\,k=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535744} {:answer/id #uuid "dbf86178-cb6d-5845-8045-a92fc476055e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,2),\\,\\,k=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535771}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dbf86178-cb6d-5845-8045-a92fc476055e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,2),\\,\\,k=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535771} :answer-field/id #uuid "43599aae-3923-57f9-9d7f-5a5e257af57c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535854}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "527b9edb-ee7b-5004-b192-96e29f02e1cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-125795" :question/problem "![](images/bf/bfcd52f31f825e6445700e8495f7acbfce46139144f8bb61e977b2971e01d877.png)\n\nThe larger polygon shown above is the image of the smaller one under the action of a dilation. Find the center of the dilation and its scale factor $k.$" :question/worked-solution "The center of a dilation lies on any line which passes through a point and its image.\n\nTherefore, we can find the center of our dilation as, for example, the point of intersection of the lines $\\overset{⟷}{B{B}^{′}}$ and $\\overset{⟷}{C{C}^{′}},$ as shown below.  \n\n![](images/d7/d7215165822a95ce9689e3666ff6ea9454048a936c372b07a6224d199b8d0ed7.png)\n\nFrom the diagram above, where we have that \n\n$$\n\\frac{{B}^{′}{C}^{′}}{BC}=\\frac{6}{2}=3,\n$$\n\nand the same proportion holds for the other corresponding sides too. As a result, the absolute value of the scale factor is \n\n$$\n|k|=3.\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, notice that our figures have different orientations, which means that the scale factor of the dilation is negative.\n\nTherefore, the scale factor is $k=-3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535925 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078520 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c2ce690-f8a9-5cc2-9baf-2d7163602589" :knowledge-point/title "The Image of a Line Under a Dilation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b33e64c-d85c-5034-8e80-bf428c69c247" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/fe/fe097fd7a569e551491d6d1ee4959af9d8d6d98e4b0bf78342f3e65c2be2e08a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535454} {:answer/id #uuid "7c8e3ce8-668b-5227-b128-873575aa7bd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/be/beb0987ba8ae5b8c2aec03fbf8a3f115beec6b2c4243944d61855c2b209ce3bc.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535628} {:answer/id #uuid "8db2ffcf-78a1-5f3d-b155-917a047be811" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/70/70e14f919d998a99c0783764952aec683074d62f842bafda05f46176079b9918.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535660} {:answer/id #uuid "a4eff6f3-1f4d-5089-a800-4d9d29a522f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/83/8380f0c8ef4c74cde183520cbe66be12fac04498ed5ea9804d36564e22f7fa61.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535692} {:answer/id #uuid "bfede5e5-f812-5503-97cf-36f17813e91c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/11/11ecad2a9413eecf0f04bffedf12d94f80e3e6a5eb45c91743e209398be28f30.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535728}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bfede5e5-f812-5503-97cf-36f17813e91c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/11/11ecad2a9413eecf0f04bffedf12d94f80e3e6a5eb45c91743e209398be28f30.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535728} :answer-field/id #uuid "6774d78d-cf72-59c0-be71-b2e0814c570a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535868}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "072c2798-6d16-5d14-9659-f337b6ce7515" :question/math-academy-id "q-125817" :question/problem "![](images/3c/3c90c2114f36f77c9bb06c47398f4f5a4ce3b8fc6aadf2a77d28c447a8e424c7.png)\n\nA dilation with center $O$ is defined by the function $(x,y)↦(1.5x,1.5y).$  What is the image of the line shown above under the action of this transformation?" :question/worked-solution "A dilation leaves a line passing through the center of the dilation unchanged.\n\nTherefore, the image ${l}^{′}$ of the line $l$ under the dilation  $(x,y)↦(1.5x,1.5y)$ is the line $l$ itself, as shown below. \n\n![](images/b4/b48fecb9d9286ac4df6a9bfbf151fd78a98d223f7375e5251cda1119f8f66611.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535997 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078518 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "261176ed-19bf-5d71-86b7-ba2e0ba31dc1" :knowledge-point/title "Dilating Figures in the Coordinate Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29429d07-f776-589c-b450-b75891b15930" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8b/8be8f57344a57823633011369efda0559726af65bac3af2e88ac5323ef653ac6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535499} {:answer/id #uuid "3fdd8d35-d9a7-5d19-b7a9-d4f954803563" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/6a/6a60e312e154995129aa750878dc41b5dfdb10d95af629e2ce96ff0469ecc63e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535531} {:answer/id #uuid "71ecab29-a47f-5134-9720-b57436f489c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/9c/9c32a2847a8027942d8b8e275b470a63920bc05ace6866cbf60bfa4bdd3ef322.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535608} {:answer/id #uuid "ba9abd05-e3b6-58c7-a525-78bff06276fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/62/62b28c3c84e9cc5d4013eef8a75bb826e71fe63cacfc42d1ef96ccdad1dd1ab8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535720} {:answer/id #uuid "ffcdbe44-93f8-5fd0-b357-ad330a45faa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3c/3c2790d0b0fe66da1c1eab58ccea08db6991c3245af4308b1dd2fcb1da06adb9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535831}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba9abd05-e3b6-58c7-a525-78bff06276fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/62/62b28c3c84e9cc5d4013eef8a75bb826e71fe63cacfc42d1ef96ccdad1dd1ab8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535720} :answer-field/id #uuid "cefe190e-880c-5995-8a4f-2586cdd0f966" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535910}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d78079e7-2f05-54ed-bf64-8723ddffd37e" :question/math-academy-id "q-88115" :question/problem "![](images/3a/3a512ef7399ac46c611335a9f957bfae9a14e802ddd39e492f6184e0e149fb7e.png)\n\nWhat is the image of the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ under a dilation that is centered at the origin and has a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "A dilation with center at the origin and a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{3}$ can be represented by the function\n\n$$\n(x,y)↦(\\frac{1}{3}x,\\frac{1}{3}y).\n$$\n\nApplying this function to the endpoints $A(-6,-3)$ and $B(3,6),$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-6,-3) & ↦(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-6),\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot -3)=(-2,-1) \\\\ (3,6) & ↦(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (3),\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 6)=(1,2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resulting points are ${A}^{′}(-2,-1)$ and ${B}^{′}(1,2).$\n\nFinally, we connect ${A}^{′}$ and ${B}^{′}$ using a segment, as shown below. \n\n![](images/ba/ba27f325ca104397544c7b5d19078d59cf63209a83d89de73cdeb752f5c0fc84.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535935 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078520 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c2ce690-f8a9-5cc2-9baf-2d7163602589" :knowledge-point/title "The Image of a Line Under a Dilation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32dc054f-2980-5bb1-b264-e8fa82722b0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7f/7ffb2de45c233357c4304386595a2a74927e6b916ab45b3e3ffdc0174c2ed720.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535509} {:answer/id #uuid "596b245e-15d7-5d45-9065-b70aba8d4afd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a4/a4f2c5660320dd58ee2fdfa6d5c9599c5d5980a4fdbea45f716261bcd48171fa.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535577} {:answer/id #uuid "63057f8d-acc1-5c02-9c3d-35b87b2aa8e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1c/1c9d1aac1d969ec9f573a5d11f547f564648c93573524fafcb20f6f5dc385b33.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535590} {:answer/id #uuid "b0c3925a-ff93-5ff6-9579-e89f60e4c383" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1a/1ab7188d164a491720d4d9d361abd4c2c76ea2cb09f6edbea3b0790a6281a785.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535706} {:answer/id #uuid "bea75270-7e93-5ea0-a4d7-4acdfe542a9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/45/459338ee2015385d68c149997a06e8789c55cd604f465d32bf3ec3ffaec2b940.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535726}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0c3925a-ff93-5ff6-9579-e89f60e4c383" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1a/1ab7188d164a491720d4d9d361abd4c2c76ea2cb09f6edbea3b0790a6281a785.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535706} :answer-field/id #uuid "634e8bd6-02be-52c8-9cbc-dcd8b0febd04" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535867}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "19060a1c-49c3-55db-84f0-fb34722058a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-89713" :question/problem "![](images/4c/4c3e53db0b578b306a12711c4e69a0d75b850cb1eecc8d5b0e6df79acc494d7a.png)\n\nA dilation with center $O$ is defined by the function $(x,y)↦(0.2x,0.2y).$  What is the image of the line shown above under the action of this transformation?" :question/worked-solution "A dilation leaves a line passing through the center of the dilation unchanged.\n\nTherefore, 